  planner_response {
    response: "# LifeOS \342\200\224 Architecture & Planning Blueprint (Step 1)\n\n> **Honest Environment & Platform Reality Check (Before We Build)**\n> This AI Studio workspace runs a **Vite + React 19 + Tailwind + Express** web preview container on port 3000. It does **not** have Xcode, Android SDK/Gradle, or an EAS native build runner inside the preview sandbox.\n> - If you want a **live, runnable, offline-first LifeOS app** you can test immediately in this preview (and install as a PWA on phone/desktop), we will build the Web/PWA client here while keeping all domain logic (`domain/`), Zod schemas, AI `LLMProvider`, and Repository interfaces 100% framework-agnostic so they drop straight into an Expo SDK repo.\n> - If you prefer an **exportable Expo SDK + React Native + NativeWind** codebase to run locally with `eas build`, we can author it for export, with the honest caveat that native modules (`@react-native-firebase/*`, `expo-local-authentication`, `expo-notifications`, camera document scanner) cannot execute inside this browser preview.\n>\n> Clarifying Question #1 below confirms which delivery path you want for Phase 1.\n\n---\n\n## 1. System Architecture Overview\n\n### Modular Clean Architecture (Single Repo, Enforced Boundaries)\nA single repository (no monorepo overhead at Stage 0) organized by feature modules with strict lint-enforced dependency direction (`ui -> hooks -> domain <- data`, never `ui -> firebase/*`):\n\n- **`core/`**: Design system tokens (Light / Dark / AMOLED), i18n (`i18next` EN / AR / Kurdish Sorani `ckb` with RTL logical properties), crypto/E2E utilities, telemetry/logger (metadata only), entitlement evaluator, error boundary.\n- **`data/` (Shared Repository Layer)**: Platform-agnostic repository interfaces (`DebtRepository`, `ReminderRepository`, `TaskRepository`, `NoteRepository`, `VaultRepository`, `HabitRepository`, `FinanceRepository`, `AuthRepository`, `StorageRepository`, `AIRepository`).\n  - **Honest Native vs. Web Firebase Split**: React Native Firebase (`@react-native-firebase/*`) wraps native Android/iOS SDKs (needed for Crashlytics, Play Integrity/App Attest, native offline cache, and reliable background behavior) and **cannot run in Expo Go**\342\200\224it requires **EAS Development Builds**. On Web, `@react-native-firebase` does not run, so we bind the **Firebase JS SDK (`firebase/*`)** using Metro/Vite platform file resolution (`*.native.ts` vs. `*.web.ts`). Both implementations satisfy the exact same TypeScript repository interfaces so UI and domain code remain 100% platform-agnostic.\n- **`features/<module>/`**: Self-contained slices for `debts`, `reminders`, `planner`, `notes`, `vault`, `habits`, `finance`, `assistant`, each split into `ui/`, `hooks/`, `domain/` (pure deterministic math/rules, 100% unit-testable), and `data/` (mappers & Zod schemas).\n- **`state/`**: Zustand stores for **ephemeral UI state only** (active filters, selected tab, modal state, biometric lock status, undo toast queue). **Cloud Firestore `onSnapshot` listeners with local persistence** serve as the single reactive source of truth for persisted data\342\200\224no TanStack Query cache duplication and no hand-rolled sync engine.\n- **`functions/`** (Firebase Cloud Functions \342\200\224 deployed only when needed): Scheduled GDPR account purge, RevenueCat webhook -> server-verified entitlements sync (Stage 1), paid-AI server proxy (Stage 1), and optional hard-cap billing guard.\n\n### Mermaid Diagram 1: High-Level System & Platform Split\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    subgraph Client[\"LifeOS Client (One Codebase: Android / iOS / Web)\"]\n        UI[\"Feature UI Layer\\n(Mobile Tabs / Web Multi-Pane, RTL, Light/Dark/AMOLED)\"]\n        UIState[\"Zustand\\n(UI State Only)\"]\n        Domain[\"Pure Domain Engines\\n(Debt Math, RRULE, 64-Notif Scheduler, Zod Schemas)\"]\n        Repo[\"Unified Repository & Auth/AI Interfaces\"]\n\n        subgraph AdapterSplit[\"Platform Adapter Split (Hidden from UI)\"]\n            NativeAdapter[\"*.native.ts\\n(@react-native-firebase + Expo SecureStore/Notifs/Camera)\"]\n            WebAdapter[\"*.web.ts\\n(Firebase JS SDK + IndexedDB + WebCrypto/PWA)\"]\n        end\n\n        UI --> UIState\n        UI --> Domain\n        UI --> Repo\n        Repo --> NativeAdapter\n        Repo --> WebAdapter\n    end\n\n    subgraph Firebase[\"Firebase Backend (Single Platform)\"]\n        AppCheck[\"Firebase App Check\\n(Play Integrity / App Attest / reCAPTCHA)\"]\n        Auth[\"Firebase Auth\\n(Google Sign-In; Apple behind flag)\"]\n        Firestore[\"Cloud Firestore\\n(Offline Persistence + Rules + Indexes)\"]\n        Storage[\"Cloud Storage\\n(Private per-user vault; optional Local-Only mode)\"]\n        RemoteConfig[\"Remote Config\\n(Model Names, Feature Flags, Plan Limits)\"]\n        AILogic[\"Firebase AI Logic\\n(Gemini Flash / Flash-Lite \342\200\224 No Key in App)\"]\n        Functions[\"Cloud Functions (Minimal)\\n(Account Purge, Stage 1 Paid-AI Proxy & Webhooks)\"]\n    end\n\n    NativeAdapter --> AppCheck\n    WebAdapter --> AppCheck\n    AppCheck --> Auth\n    AppCheck --> Firestore\n    AppCheck --> Storage\n    AppCheck --> RemoteConfig\n    AppCheck --> AILogic\n    Functions --> Firestore\n    Functions --> Storage\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Firestore Data Model, Indexes & Offline Sync Strategy\n\n### Per-User Isolated Hierarchy (`users/{uid}/...`)\nZero cross-user collection paths. Every document includes `version: number`, `createdAt: Timestamp`, `updatedAt: Timestamp`, and `deletedAt: Timestamp | null` (soft-delete tombstone for safe offline reconciliation).\n\n| Collection Path | Key Fields & Denormalization | Purpose & Read-Optimization |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `users/{uid}` | `displayName`, `locale` (`en`/`ar`/`ckb`), `defaultCurrency`, `theme`, `aiSettings` (global + per-module toggles), `consent` (analytics/AI timestamps), `quietHours`, `e2eEnabled` | 1 doc loaded on boot. |\n| `users/{uid}/entitlements/current` | `plan` (`free`/`pro`/`family`), `limits` (`aiDailyQuota`, `storageMb`, `maxDocs`, `e2eVault`), `expiresAt`, `source` | **Client read-only**; written only by Admin/Cloud Functions. |\n| `users/{uid}/debtLedgers/{personKey}` | `personName`, `contactId`, `totalsByCurrency: Record<Currency, {iOwe, owedToMe}>`, `onTimeCount`, `lateCount`, `activeDebtCount` | **Denormalized aggregate** updated atomically via Firestore Transactions when debts/payments change. Powers dashboard & deterministic repayment probability with 1 read per person. |\n| `users/{uid}/debts/{debtId}` | `direction` (`i_owe`/`owed_to_me`), `personKey`, `personName`, `principalMinor` (integer minor units to avoid float bugs), `remainingMinor`, `currency`, `fxSnapshot`, `interestBps`, `startDate`, `dueDate`, `status` (`active`/`partial`/`overdue`/`settled`), `installmentPlan`, `notes`, `attachmentPaths[]` | Paginated by `status` + `dueDate`. All balances in integer minor units (e.g., cents / IQD dinars). |\n| `users/{uid}/debts/{debtId}/payments/{paymentId}` | `amountMinor`, `paidAt`, `method`, `note`, `idempotencyKey`, `receiptPath` | Append-only ledger; mutating parent `remainingMinor` & `debtLedgers` in a single atomic transaction. |\n| `users/{uid}/reminders/{reminderId}` | `title`, `category`, `icon`, `color`, `rrule`, `timezone`, `nextTriggerAt`, `snoozedUntil`, `linkedEntity` (`{type, id}`), `isCompleted`, `completionHistory[]` (capped last 20) | Indexed by `isCompleted` + `nextTriggerAt` for the rolling 64-notification scheduler. |\n| `users/{uid}/taskLists/{listId}` | `name`, `color`, `icon`, `sortKey` (fractional index / LexoRank), `archived` | Single small collection (<50 docs). |\n| `users/{uid}/tasks/{taskId}` | `listId`, `title`, `subtasks[]` (max 30 embedded), `priority` (1\342\200\2234), `tags[]`, `dueDate`, `rrule`, `sortKey`, `status`, `linkedReminderId` | Fractional `sortKey` means drag-and-drop reorders cost **1 write**, never N writes. |\n| `users/{uid}/noteFolders/{folderId}` | `name`, `parentId`, `color`, `isLocked`, `sortKey` | Unlimited nesting via `parentId` adjacency list. |\n| `users/{uid}/notes/{noteId}` | `folderId`, `title`, `content` (or `ciphertext` + `iv` if E2E), `isEncrypted`, `isLocked`, `pinned`, `color`, `tags[]`, `links[]`, `searchTokens[]` | `versions/{verId}` subcollection stores snapshots (client prunes >10 versions). |\n| `users/{uid}/vaultFolders/{folderId}` | `name`, `parentId`, `isLocked`, `isEncrypted`, `sortKey` | Nested folder tree for Document Vault. |\n| `users/{uid}/documents/{docId}` | `folderId`, `title`, `docType` (`passport`/`id`/`invoice`/`contract`/`receipt`/`other`), `storageMode` (`cloud`/`local_only`), `storagePath`, `thumbPath`, `mimeType`, `sizeBytes`, `expiryDate`, `isEncrypted`, `ocrTextSnippet`, `extractedFields`, `favorite` | Never stores raw binary in Firestore (1MB limit). Short-lived download URLs generated on demand. |\n| `users/{uid}/habits/{habitId}` | `name`, `frequency`, `targetPerPeriod`, `currentStreak`, `bestStreak`, `reminderTime`, `color`, `archived` | Typically 5\342\200\22320 docs. |\n| `users/{uid}/habitLogs/{yyyy_mm}` | `entries: Record<habitId, Record<dayNum, {done, value, ts}>>` | **Monthly bucketed document**: cuts 365 docs/year/habit down to **12 docs/year total** across all habits! |\n| `users/{uid}/transactions/{txId}` | `type` (`income`/`expense`), `amountMinor`, `currency`, `category`, `occurredAt`, `merchant`, `subscriptionId`, `note` | Paginated by `occurredAt desc`. Monthly summary cached in `financeSummaries/{yyyy_mm}`. |\n| `users/{uid}/subscriptions/{subId}` | `name`, `amountMinor`, `currency`, `cadence`, `nextRenewalAt`, `reminderId`, `active` | Low cardinality (<50 docs). |\n| `users/{uid}/aiUsage/{yyyy_mm_dd}` | `requestCount`, `inputTokensEst`, `outputTokensEst`, `lastRequestAt` | Daily per-user quota ledger. |\n| `users/{uid}/aiCache/{hashKey}` | `promptVersion`, `responseJson`, `expiresAt` | Avoids paying quota/tokens for identical parses/summaries. |\n\n### Required Composite Indexes (`firestore.indexes.json`)\n1. `debts`: `(deletedAt ASC, status ASC, dueDate ASC)` \342\200\224 active/overdue dashboard & calendar.\n2. `debts`: `(deletedAt ASC, personKey ASC, updatedAt DESC)` \342\200\224 per-person ledger detail.\n3. `reminders`: `(deletedAt ASC, isCompleted ASC, nextTriggerAt ASC)` \342\200\224 rolling 64-notification window query.\n4. `tasks`: `(deletedAt ASC, status ASC, dueDate ASC)` \342\200\224 Today / Upcoming / Calendar unified view.\n5. `tasks`: `(deletedAt ASC, listId ASC, sortKey ASC)` \342\200\224 ordered list view.\n6. `notes`: `(deletedAt ASC, folderId ASC, pinned DESC, updatedAt DESC)` \342\200\224 folder view.\n7. `documents`: `(deletedAt ASC, expiryDate ASC)` \342\200\224 expiring documents briefing & alerts.\n8. `documents`: `(deletedAt ASC, folderId ASC, updatedAt DESC)` \342\200\224 vault folder navigation.\n9. `transactions`: `(deletedAt ASC, occurredAt DESC)` & `(deletedAt ASC, category ASC, occurredAt DESC)` \342\200\224 finance charts & budgets.\n\n### Conflict Resolution & Offline Reconciliation Strategy\n- **Default entities (Notes, Tasks, Reminders, Habits, Vault metadata)**: Last-Write-Wins (LWW) using `serverTimestamp()` on `updatedAt` plus monotonic `version` increment. Soft deletes (`deletedAt`) prevent offline edits on Device B from resurrecting a record deleted on Device A.\n- **Financial & Debt Ledgers (High integrity)**: **Append-only payments** with client-generated UUID `idempotencyKey` (`payments/{idempotencyKey}`) + Firestore `runTransaction` to recompute `remainingMinor` and `debtLedgers` when online. If recorded offline, queued deterministically and reconciled without double-counting because the document ID is the idempotency key.\n- **Habit Logs**: Field-level `merge: true` dot-notation updates (`entries.<habitId>.<day>`) so completing Habit A on phone and Habit B on web offline never overwrite each other.\n\n### Mermaid Diagram 2: Offline Sync & Rolling 64-Notification Scheduler\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant U as User Action\n    participant Repo as Repository Layer\n    participant Cache as Firestore Local Cache\n    participant Cloud as Cloud Firestore\n    participant Sched as Rolling 64-Notif Engine\n\n    U->>Repo: Create/Update Reminder or Debt Payment\n    Repo->>Repo: Validate with Zod + Generate idempotencyKey\n    Repo->>Cache: Optimistic Write (works 100% offline)\n    Cache-->>U: Instant UI update via onSnapshot\n    Repo->>Sched: Recompute next 64 triggers (RRULE + Quiet Hours)\n    Sched->>Sched: Diff vs scheduled OS IDs -> Cancel stale / Schedule new\n    Note over Cache,Cloud: When connectivity returns\n    Cache->>Cloud: Sync queued mutations (Transactions for Ledgers)\n    Cloud-->>Cache: serverTimestamp() & reconciled state\n    Cache-->>Sched: Trigger background reschedule on remote changes/timezone/boot\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Security Rules, Privacy & STRIDE-Lite Threat Model\n\n### Firestore & Storage Security Rules Architecture\n- **Master Gate**: Deny all by default (`allow read, write: if false;`). Every match requires `request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == uid`.\n- **Server-Only Entitlements**: `users/{uid}/entitlements/{doc}` allows `read` for owner, **`write: if false`** (only Admin SDK / Cloud Functions may grant Pro/Family).\n- **Schema & Size Enforcement**: Every `create`/`update` validates `request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(...)`, exact field types, string max lengths (e.g., `title.size() <= 200`, `note.size() <= 100000`), positive integer minor units, valid currency/status enums, and immutability of `createdAt` and `uid`.\n- **Quota Tamper Resistance**: On Spark (client-only), `aiUsage/{day}` rules enforce monotonic increments (`requestCount == resource.data.requestCount + 1`) and deny deletes so a modified client cannot reset its own daily counter. In Stage 1 (Cloud Function AI proxy), quota enforcement moves 100% server-side.\n- **Cloud Storage Rules**: Path strictly `users/{uid}/vault/{docId}/{fileName}`. Enforces `request.auth.uid == uid`, `request.resource.size <= 10 * 1024 * 1024` (Free) / `50MB` (Pro), and MIME allowlist (`application/pdf`, `image/jpeg`, `image/png`, `image/webp`, `application/octet-stream` for E2E `.enc` blobs). No public reads ever.\n- **Rules Unit Tests**: Automated test suite using `@firebase/rules-unit-testing` against the Firebase Emulator Suite proving: (a) User B cannot read/list/write User A\'s paths, (b) client cannot elevate `entitlements/current`, (c) oversized or malformed payloads fail, (d) immutable fields cannot be altered.\n\n### STRIDE-Lite Threat Model\n\n| Threat | Concrete Attack Vector | Mitigation in LifeOS |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Spoofing** | Stolen session or unauthorized app binary calling Firestore/AI Logic | Firebase Auth + App Check (Play Integrity / App Attest / reCAPTCHA) enforced on Firestore, Storage, and AI Logic; re-authentication required before export, E2E key reveal, or account delete. |\n| **Tampering** | Client alters `entitlements` to Pro, resets `aiUsage`, or injects negative payment | Rules block client writes to `entitlements`; monotonic rules on `aiUsage`; Zod + Rules validate numeric ranges and immutable fields; payment amounts strictly `> 0`. |\n| **Repudiation** | Disputed debt balance or accidental deletion | Append-only `payments` subcollection with `createdAt` server timestamps, soft-delete tombstones, and undo toast windows. |\n| **Information Disclosure** | Lost unlocked phone, screenshot leak, or free-tier AI retaining passport data | Biometric/PIN app lock + auto-lock timer; `FLAG_SECURE` / screen-capture shield on Vault & locked notes; secure clipboard auto-clear; optional AES-256-GCM E2E encryption (keys in Keychain/Keystore + 24-word/hex recovery key); **Hard Privacy Gate** blocking sensitive payloads while `free_tier=true`. |\n| **Denial of Service** | Bot spamming free-tier Gemini AI or flooding Storage | App Check + daily per-user quota + exponential backoff on 429 + Storage file size/count caps + GCP budget alerts. |\n| **Elevation / Prompt Injection** | Malicious text inside a scanned invoice/note instructs Global AI to delete debts or send data | Strict untrusted-data framing (`<untrusted_document>`); tool-calling sandbox where **every mutating action** renders a mandatory interactive confirmation preview card before executing. |\n\n### E2E Encryption Trade-Offs (Honest Summary)\n- **How it works**: Opt-in per Vault folder / locked note. Client derives a Master Vault Key (MVK) using PBKDF2/Argon2 from user passphrase + random salt, wraps MVK in device Keychain/Keystore (unlocked via biometrics), and generates a printable/exportable **Recovery Key**. Files/notes are encrypted client-side with AES-256-GCM before upload.\n- **Explicit Trade-Offs**: (1) If the user loses both device and Recovery Key, data is **permanently unrecoverable**\342\200\224Firebase/support cannot reset it. (2) Server-side search and Cloud AI **cannot read E2E content** unless the user explicitly decrypts a selected snippet on-device and approves sending it (still blocked while `free_tier=true`).\n\n---\n\n## 4. AI Architecture, Privacy Gate & Semantic Search\n\n### Provider-Agnostic Design (`LLMProvider`)\nAll AI features talk to a single interface: `generateText()`, `generateStructured<T>(zodSchema)`, and `runToolTurn()`.\n- **Stage 0 Implementation**: `FirebaseAILogicProvider` using Firebase AI Logic SDK (Gemini Developer API free tier, protected by App Check, **zero API key bundled in the app**). Model names (`ai.model.primary = gemini-2.5-flash`, `ai.model.lite = gemini-2.5-flash-lite`) live in **Firebase Remote Config**, never hardcoded.\n- **Stage 1 Switch**: Swap to `CloudFunctionProxyProvider` pointing at a Firebase Cloud Function (Vertex AI / paid Gemini / Claude / OpenAI) via Remote Config\342\200\224**zero UI or module code changes**.\n\n### Deterministic Math Boundary (Non-Negotiable)\nThe LLM **never** computes numbers, dates, or probabilities:\n- **Debt Math**: Remaining balances, multi-currency totals, interest accrual, snowball vs. avalanche payoff schedules, cash-flow affordability date, and **repayment probability** (deterministic Beta-smoothed historical on-time ratio + overdue-days decay) are computed in pure, unit-tested TypeScript functions. The LLM receives the computed JSON summary only to write a human explanation or draft polite EN/AR/KU reminder messages.\n- **Recurrence & Scheduling**: RRULE expansion, conflict detection, and optimal reminder-time histograms run deterministically on-device.\n\n### Mermaid Diagram 3: AI Request Pipeline, Privacy Gate & Fallback Chain\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    Req[\"Module AI Request\\n(Prompt Version + Payload)\"] --> Toggle{\"Global & Module\\nAI Enabled?\"}\n    Toggle -- No --> NonAI[\"Graceful Non-AI UX\"]\n    Toggle -- Yes --> Gate{\"PRIVACY GATE\\n(free_tier == true?)\"}\n    Gate -- \"Sensitive: Vault Image,\\nID/Passport, Locked/E2E\" --> Block[\"BLOCKED\\nShow Notice & Local/On-Device Path\"]\n    Gate -- \"Allowed Minimal\\nUser-Selected Text\" --> Cache{\"aiCache Hit?\"}\n    Cache -- Yes --> Return[\"Return Validated JSON\"]\n    Cache -- No --> Quota{\"Daily Quota\\nRemaining?\"}\n    Quota -- No --> NonAI\n    Quota -- Yes --> Primary[\"Primary Model\\n(Remote Config: Flash)\"]\n    Primary -- \"429 / Timeout\\n(Exp. Backoff)\" --> Lite[\"Fallback Model\\n(Remote Config: Flash-Lite)\"]\n    Lite -- Fail --> NonAI\n    Primary -- OK --> Zod[\"Zod Schema Validation\\n+ Prompt Injection Guard\"]\n    Lite -- OK --> Zod\n    Zod --> Mutate{\"Mutates User Data?\"}\n    Mutate -- Yes --> Confirm[\"Mandatory User\\nConfirmation Preview\"]\n    Mutate -- No --> Return\n```\n\n### Semantic Search / RAG Justification\n- **Choice**: **Hybrid Architecture behind a `SearchProvider` interface**.\n  - **Stage 0 (Free Tier / Offline-First)**: On-device encrypted local search index (tokenized BM25 + multilingual EN/AR/KU normalization + metadata/tag filters + on-device OCR text for non-E2E docs). Zero server cost, works 100% offline, zero privacy leakage, and honors the `free_tier=true` privacy gate.\n  - **Stage 1 (Paid / Pro Tier)**: Add **Firestore Vector Search (`findNearest`)** scoped strictly under `users/{uid}/embeddings/{itemId}` (single-user isolation enforced by Security Rules), populated only for non-E2E items when `free_tier=false` and user opts in. Avoids shipping 80\342\200\223150MB ONNX embedding models in the mobile bundle while keeping everything inside Firebase (no Pinecone/Weaviate).\n\n### \"Upgrade AI Provider\" Checklist (Stage 0 -> Stage 1)\n1. Enable paid billing on Vertex AI / Gemini API (or Anthropic/OpenAI) with **Zero Data Retention (ZDR)** / no-training enterprise terms.\n2. Deploy `aiProxy` Cloud Function with Firebase Auth verification, App Check enforcement, server-side per-plan quota enforcement, and Secret Manager API keys.\n3. Update Remote Config: switch `ai.provider = \"cloud_proxy\"`, set `ai.free_tier = false`, configure per-plan quotas (`free: 15/day`, `pro: 250/day`, `family: 600/day`).\n4. Unlock Cloud Document AI (passport/invoice field extraction) behind explicit per-feature user consent.\n5. Configure GCP Billing Budget Alerts (50% / 80% / 100%) and Cloud Function concurrency/rate caps.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Risk Register & Honest Platform Limitations\n\n| Risk / Limitation | Honest Reality & Impact | Concrete Mitigation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Cloud Storage requires Blaze plan** | Firebase Spark no longer offers free Cloud Storage buckets without a billing account (Blaze still includes free monthly allowances: 5GB storage, 1GB/day download). | Offer a first-class **\"Local-Only Document Vault\"** mode (zero billing required). When enabling Blaze, configure GCP Budget Alerts + a Pub/Sub-triggered Cloud Function kill-switch / Storage rules cap. |\n| **EAS Development Builds (No Expo Go)** | Native `@react-native-firebase/*`, ML Kit OCR, and camera edge-scanning cannot run inside Expo Go. | Use EAS Development Builds (`eas build --profile development`); Android APKs build free on EAS free tier. |\n| **iOS Distribution Without $99/yr Apple Account** | Personal iPhone sideloading via free Apple ID expires every **7 days** (requires Xcode rebuild). Web PWA on iOS Safari works indefinitely for free, **but** iOS PWAs have limited background sync, no silent local scheduled notifications without Web Push, and no native biometric Keychain API. | Document both paths honestly: Android gets full native APK free; iOS personal use uses Web PWA (or 7-day dev build) until ready for Apple Developer Program ($99/yr). |\n| **Apple Sign-In Mandate (Stage 1)** | Apple App Store Review Guideline 4.8 **requires** Sign in with Apple if you ship Google Sign-In on iOS. | Build `AuthProvider` abstraction now with Remote Config flag `auth.apple.enabled = false` + full setup runbook (Apple Developer account, Services ID, `.p8` key, Firebase Auth config). |\n| **iOS 64 Local Notification Cap & Custom Icons** | iOS hard-caps pending local notifications at **64** per app and does not support arbitrary per-notification status-bar icons (only notification attachments/avatars). Web browsers cannot reliably schedule offline local alarms. | Build a unit-tested **Rolling-Window Scheduler** that keeps the next 64 occurrences across all RRULEs queued and refreshes on app open, completion, timezone change, and background fetch. On Android, use distinct Notification Channels + large icons; on iOS, use rich image attachments & category actions; on Web, show clear in-app banners + optional Notification API while open. |\n| **Firestore Cost Traps** | Unbounded collection listeners, drag-and-drop sort rewrites, per-day habit documents, and chatty dashboard queries can blow past 50k reads/day even with few users. | Denormalized `debtLedgers` & `financeSummaries`, monthly bucketed `habitLogs`, fractional `sortKey` (1 write per drag), cursor pagination (25 items/page), and bounded `onSnapshot` queries (`limit()` + `deletedAt == null`). |\n| **Firebase Vendor Lock-In** | Firestore queries and Rules are Firebase-specific. | Repository pattern isolates all Firebase SDK calls; full user-initiated JSON + ZIP export ensures complete data portability. |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Cost Model & Unit Economics\n\n### Typical Active User Daily Firestore Budget (Optimized Architecture)\n- App boot (profile + entitlements + active ledgers + today\'s tasks/reminders + current month habit log + finance summary): **~18\342\200\22325 reads** (cached locally after first load; subsequent opens read only deltas!).\n- Typical daily mutations (3 tasks, 4 habit ticks, 1 transaction, 1 debt payment, 2 AI quota ticks): **~15\342\200\22325 writes**, **~40\342\200\22360 total reads/day** across devices.\n- **Stage 0 (1 user)**: ~60 reads / 25 writes per day = **< 0.15% of Spark free tier** (50k reads / 20k writes daily). **$0/month.**\n\n### Monthly Scaling Cost Estimate (Paid Tiers at Scale)\n\n| Scale | Est. Firestore | Est. Storage (avg 25MB/user) | Est. Cloud Functions & Hosting | Est. Paid AI (Flash/Lite, 80% Free / 20% Pro) | **Total Est. / mo** | **Cost / Active User** |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Stage 0 (1 user)** | $0 (Spark) | $0 (Local-only or Blaze free tier) | $0 | $0 (AI Logic free tier) | **$0** | **$0.00** |\n| **1,000 MAU** | ~$1\342\200\223$3 | ~$1 | ~$1 | ~$8\342\200\223$15 | **~$11\342\200\223$20** | **~$0.015** |\n| **10,000 MAU** | ~$25\342\200\223$45 | ~$12\342\200\223$20 | ~$8\342\200\223$15 | ~$90\342\200\223$160 | **~$135\342\200\223$240** | **~$0.019** |\n| **100,000 MAU** | ~$320\342\200\223$550 | ~$150\342\200\223$280 | ~$90\342\200\223$180 | ~$950\342\200\223$1,700 | **~$1,510\342\200\223$2,710** | **~$0.021** |\n\n- **Suggested Stage 1 Pricing**: **Free** (core offline modules, 50MB vault, 10\342\200\22315 Flash-Lite AI actions/day) | **Pro** ($5.99/mo or $49.99/yr \342\200\224 5GB vault, E2E encryption, 250 AI actions/day, Document OCR/AI extraction, advanced forecasts) | **Family** ($9.99/mo up to 5 isolated accounts). Even at 4% Pro conversion, 100k MAU yields **~$20k\342\200\223$24k MRR** vs. **~$2.7k infra cost** (~87% gross margin).\n- **Optimization Inflection Point (~20,000\342\200\22335,000 MAU)**: Move heavy document storage egress behind Cloud CDN / Cloudflare R2 if media bandwidth dominates, enforce strict AI response caching & token caps, and archive settled debts/transactions >18 months old into cold summary bundles.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Phased Delivery Roadmap (Phases 1\342\200\2237)\n\n| Phase | Scope & Deliverables | Automated Verification & Exit Gate |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Phase 1: Core Foundation** | Project scaffold, Design System tokens (Light/Dark/AMOLED), responsive shell (mobile tabs / web sidebar), i18n + RTL (EN/AR/Kurdish Sorani), Auth abstraction (Google Sign-In + Apple flag), Firestore offline persistence + Repository layer, `firebase-blueprint.json` + hardened `firestore.rules` & Storage rules + Emulator Rules unit tests, App Check wiring, `LLMProvider` skeleton (Remote Config model routing, quotas, cache, Privacy Gate), CI workflow. | Rules unit tests pass (cross-user denied), pure core tests pass, build & lint clean, live auth + offline write verified. |\n| **Phase 2: Debts Manager (+ AI)** | Multi-currency debts (\"I owe\" / \"Owed to me\"), integer minor-unit ledger math, partial payments with idempotent transactions, installment plans, per-person ledger & charts. Deterministic repayment probability, snowball/avalanche planner, cash-flow forecast, AI NL entry + multilingual polite reminder drafter (EN/AR/KU). | 95%+ unit-test coverage on debt math, FX snapshot, payoff planner, and probability engine; end-to-end payment & offline reconciliation verified. |\n| **Phase 3: Smart Reminders (+ AI)** | Recurrence engine (RRULE), 8 categories with icons/colors/channels, quiet hours, snooze/done actions, birthday contacts import, **Rolling 64-Notification Scheduler**. AI NL scheduling, auto-categorization, schedule overload detection & optimal-time heuristics. | Unit tests covering RRULE edge cases, DST/timezone shifts, quiet hours, and strict `<= 64` rolling queue cap. |\n| **Phase 4: To-Do/Planner & Notes (+ AI)** | Projects/lists, tasks/subtasks, fractional-index drag-and-drop, Today/Upcoming/Completed + unified calendar (tasks + reminders + debt due dates). Rich Markdown notes, nested folders, checklists, pinning, biometric lock, version history, cross-module links. AI goal breakdown, \"Plan my day\", rewrite/translate/extract tasks, local semantic/hybrid search. | Drag-and-drop sort-key tests, Markdown/link tests, AI confirmation-preview guard tests. |\n| **Phase 5: Document Vault (+ AI)** | Document capture/upload (crop/enhance/PDF pipeline), unlimited nested folders, expiry reminders, biometric lock, Local-Only vs. Private Cloud Storage mode, opt-in AES-256-GCM E2E encryption + recovery key flow. On-device OCR/text indexing; Cloud Document AI strictly gated behind `free_tier=false`. | Crypto encrypt/decrypt + recovery key unit tests; Privacy Gate unit tests proving `free_tier=true` blocks Vault payloads. |\n| **Phase 6: Habits, Finance & Global Assistant** | Habits with streaks, heatmaps (monthly bucketed logs), haptics, break-analysis. Finance transactions, budgets, recurring subscriptions, CSV import/export, forecast. Global Command Palette + Floating AI Assistant with cross-module tool calling (confirmation preview required), Home AI Daily Briefing, Global Search. | Habit streak & monthly-bucket merge tests, CSV round-trip tests, Finance forecast tests, Assistant confirmation-gate tests. |\n| **Phase 7: Monetization Scaffolding, Hardening & Release Prep** | Server-verified entitlements evaluator, Paywall & Restore Purchases scaffolding, RevenueCat/Stripe extension docs, GDPR full JSON/ZIP export & account purge flow, STRIDE security audit, performance & accessibility (WCAG AA + RTL) audit, backup/restore & Upgrade AI Provider runbooks. | Full CI suite (lint, typecheck, unit tests, Rules tests), WCAG/RTL audit, restore drill verified. |\n\n---\n\nPlease answer the **5 critical clarifying questions** below so we can lock in the exact configuration and begin Phase 1."
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Core Request**\n\nI\'m taking a step back to thoroughly dissect the user\'s ambitious request. The goal is clear: a \"LifeOS\" built with the Expo ecosystem. The technical specifications look comprehensive.\n\n\n**Reviewing the Project Context**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the operational environment and user directives. The React SPA on Vite + Express is crucial context. I\'m also carefully aligning my approach with the user\'s \"NON-NEGOTIABLE PRINCIPLES,\" particularly the emphasis on honesty, risk assessment, and the upfront architectural design phase. The directives about code-writing are super clear!\n\n\n**Reconciling Mode and Directive**\n\nI\'m wrestling with a potential conflict. I need to deliver all of Step 1\'s deliverables, like Mermaid diagrams and a risk list, BEFORE firing any clarifying questions. My planning mode is clear. It\'s a key directive to not ask any questions until the requested plan is provided. This also aligns with the `ask_question` tool description.\n\n\n**Clarifying Constraints & Directives**\n\nI\'m now grappling with some confusing directives. I\'m focusing on the conflicting instructions around plan creation and question-asking. I see I must not produce an implementation plan immediately. My current plan is to yield after posing questions so the user can interact. I\'m making sure I don\'t give the user any actual code. I need to make sure I deliver *only* the Step 1 deliverables.\n\n\n**Evaluating the Output Sequence**\n\nI\'m now deeply concerned about the order of operations. The user\'s directive is very clear: deliver the Step 1 deliverables *before* asking questions. My initial impulse was to ask questions first, but that would violate the user\'s explicit instructions. However, if I output the deliverables and *then* ask clarifying questions via the `ask_question` tool, will it work properly? I need to clarify what the tool does after a text response.\n\n\n**Confirming Tool Functionality**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the `ask_question` tool\'s behavior. I re-read its documentation and it confirmed my suspicions! The tool is designed to work *after* providing contextual information in the text response, which is perfect for addressing the situation at hand. It also reminds me to be 100% honest about the limitations of the AI Studio Build environment, which is an important operating principle.\n\n**Addressing Runtime Environment Conflicts**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining a critical platform conflict: the difference between the Vite + Express web app sandbox and the desired Expo/React Native environment. This is a very important operating principle. This reveals two clear options for my initial question! Option A is a live, runnable web app and Option B is an architecture that works for Expo.\n\n\n**Defining the Architecture Options**\n\nI\'m now carefully developing the two architectural options. Option A is a testable, offline-first React + TypeScript + Tailwind PWA, runnable in this workspace using the Firebase JS SDK. Option B involves authoring Expo SDK, React Native, and other native dependencies for export and local compilation, as I will be honest that this preview won\'t compile all native modules. This is aligned with my operating principles.\n\n\n**Validating Delivery Completeness**\n\nI\'m now meticulously reviewing all requested deliverables for completeness and quality, to make sure I deliver on the promise. I need to ensure the architecture delivers on all facets of the plan, with no omissions or compromises. I\'m focusing on principal-architect quality, honesty, and accuracy. This ensures all items requested in Step 1 are accounted for and well-explained.\n\n\n**Detailing the Technical Specifications**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into technical specifications. I am designing around Firestore\'s real-time listeners for reactive data, along with React Hook Form and Zod schemas for validation. I\'m also planning the Design System and UX with Material You, iOS tokens, and RTL support, alongside responsive layouts and WCAG AA accessibility. I am focusing on STRIDE-lite threats, device security, and GDPR/CCPA compliance. Furthermore, I will create Monetization-Ready Scaffolding.\n\n\n**Refining the Deliverables**\n\nI\'m now refining the Step 1 deliverables for clarity and thoroughness. I\'m focusing on the diagram content: App Check, AI Logic, the Privacy Gate, and the complete offline-first sync. Also, I\'m defining the database model, including the strict isolation under `users/{uid}/...` subcollections. I\'m taking the opportunity to detail how to reconcile conflicts.\n\n\n**Elaborating Database Schema**\n\nI\'m now detailing the database schema, including the use of subcollections like `users/{uid}/notes/{noteId}` and `users/{uid}/vaultFolders/{folderId}`. I\'m also planning the composite indexes. I\'m focusing on the conflict resolution strategy, including `updatedAt`, `version`, and soft deletes. I\'m also adding semantic search architectural considerations!\n\n\n**Finalizing Security Principles**\n\nI\'m now detailing Firebase security rules. I\'m focusing on least privilege, immutability of critical fields, and strict validation using Zod schemas. I\'m also planning Cloud Storage private path isolation and MIME allowlists. I\'m ensuring short-lived signed URLs and testing using `@firebase/rules-unit-testing`. I\'m aligning these rules with the overall architecture.\n\n\n**Finalizing Threat Analysis**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the threat model, expanding the STRIDE-lite analysis. This includes detailing cross-user access controls, and also oversized payload tests, to ensure robustness. The final pieces are the LLMProvider abstraction and model selection via Remote Config, alongside a deterministic-vs-LLM boundary. I\'m focusing on the Privacy Gate, prompt-injection defense, and the \"Upgrade AI Provider\" checklist.\n\n\n**Refining Mitigation Strategies**\n\nI\'m now detailing Firebase vendor lock-in mitigation, focusing on a repository pattern and full JSON/ZIP export. I\'m also elaborating on Firestore cost traps, including architectural mitigations for unbounded listeners and collection group scans. I\'m carefully analyzing Cloud Storage cost risks, outlining budget alerts and a \"Local-Only Vault\" mode. EAS Dev Build\'s limitations are now clear, especially regarding iOS\'s personal distribution and notification constraints, alongside Apple Sign-In requirements.\n\n\n**Formulating Final Queries**\n\nI\'m now crafting the final clarifying questions, keeping in mind the strict requirements of the `ask_question` tool. I must make sure the questions are simple, plain text, and focus on essential areas. They must be concise and targeted to ensure I receive the information I need, given the user\'s constraints. I\'ll focus on information that is critical to making good decisions.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Crucial Questions**\n\nI\'m now finalizing the questions for the `ask_question` tool. I\'m focusing on five high-impact architectural decisions that require the user\'s input before Phase 1. I\'m prioritizing the questions for brevity and clarity, with pre-written options to expedite the process. The focus is on the target runtime for Phase 1 in the workspace, the Firebase project, billing posture, default currency, and localization.\n\n\n**Finalizing Project Scope**\n\nI\'m now carefully consolidating my questions for the user. I\'ve focused my queries on key decisions impacting the initial architecture. Specifically, I\'m asking for their preference for visual themes across different modes, and the desired E2E scope for sensitive data, with recommended options provided. I\'m also ready to execute the tool!\n\n\n"
