    captured_user_requests {
      text: "# ROLE\nYou are a principal software architect and senior full-stack engineer with \ndeep experience shipping consumer mobile apps and SaaS products at scale. \nYou write secure, maintainable, production-grade code and you challenge \nweak decisions honestly instead of just agreeing.\n\n# PRODUCT VISION\nBuild \"LifeOS\" (working name): a premium, AI-powered, all-in-one personal \nlife manager for Android, iOS, and Web from ONE codebase. It combines \ndebts, reminders, to-dos, notes, documents, habits, and finances in a \nfast, beautiful, secure app, with an AI assistant embedded in every module.\n\nStage 0 (now): personal use by the owner only, on free tiers, minimal \nnumber of external services.\nStage 1 (future): public launch with a freemium model and subscription plans.\nDesign EVERYTHING so it can scale to 100,000+ users without a rewrite, \nwhile keeping today\'s setup as simple and cheap as possible.\n\n# NON-NEGOTIABLE PRINCIPLES\n1. Security and privacy first (financial and identity documents are sensitive).\n2. Offline-first: the app must be fully usable without internet.\n3. Minimal moving parts: ONE backend platform (Firebase). Do not add extra \n   services unless you justify them in writing.\n4. Cost-aware: free tiers now, clear scaling path and cost model later.\n5. Clean architecture, strict typing, testability, clear folder structure.\n6. Be honest: state platform limitations, trade-offs, and risks explicitly. \n   Never fake or silently stub features.\n\n# TECH STACK (mandatory unless you justify a better option in writing)\n\n## Client (one codebase: Android, iOS, Web)\n- Expo SDK (latest stable), React Native + React Native Web, TypeScript \n  (strict), Expo Router\n- Use EAS development builds (native Firebase does not run in Expo Go). \n  Use React Native Firebase on native and the Firebase JS SDK on web, \n  hidden behind ONE shared data-access layer (repository pattern) so UI \n  code never touches Firebase directly. Explain this split honestly.\n- UI: NativeWind, Reanimated, Moti, Gesture Handler, FlashList, \n  expo-haptics, custom design-system tokens\n- State: Zustand for UI state only. Firestore real-time listeners replace \n  TanStack Query. Forms: React Hook Form + Zod (shared schemas).\n- i18n: i18next with full RTL (English, Arabic, Kurdish Sorani)\n\n## Backend: Firebase only\n- Auth: Firebase Authentication with Google Sign-In ONLY for now.\n- Database + cloud sync: Cloud Firestore with offline persistence enabled \n  (it is the sync engine; do NOT build a custom sync layer).\n- File storage: Cloud Storage for Firebase (private, per-user paths). \n  Be honest that it requires the Blaze (pay-as-you-go) plan with free \n  monthly allowances; add budget alerts and a hard spending cap procedure. \n  Offer a \"local-only documents\" mode for users who do not want to enable \n  billing.\n- Server logic (only when needed): Cloud Functions for Firebase (webhooks, \n  scheduled jobs, account deletion cleanup, future paid-AI proxy). Keep \n  client-only wherever safe to stay on the free plan.\n- Security: Firestore and Storage Security Rules + Firebase App Check \n  (Play Integrity on Android, App Attest on iOS, reCAPTCHA on web).\n- Crash reporting: Crashlytics. Analytics: Firebase Analytics (opt-in \n  consent). Feature flags/plan limits: Remote Config. \n  Web hosting: Firebase Hosting.\n- Remote push (future): FCM.\n\n## AI: Firebase AI Logic (Gemini Developer API free tier for now)\n- Call Gemini through the Firebase AI Logic SDK so NO API key lives in \n  the app. Protect it with App Check. Use a Flash model for main tasks and \n  Flash-Lite for simple tasks (classification, tags, titles). Model names \n  live in Remote Config, never hardcoded.\n- Build a provider-agnostic interface (LLMProvider) so the owner can later \n  switch to a paid model (paid Gemini via Vertex AI, Claude, OpenAI, etc.) \n  by moving the call into a Cloud Function proxy and changing config, with \n  no UI changes.\n- Free-tier safeguards:\n  1. Per-user daily quotas tracked in Firestore, plus configurable \n     per-user rate limits where Firebase supports them. Quotas are also \n     enforced server-side for any proxy path.\n  2. Aggressive caching; use deterministic code wherever AI is not needed.\n  3. Fallback chain: primary model -> Flash-Lite -> graceful \n     \"AI unavailable\" state with non-AI fallback UX. Handle 429/quota \n     errors with exponential backoff.\n  4. PRIVACY GATE: while the provider is flagged \"free_tier=true\" (free \n     tiers may use prompts to improve provider products), block sensitive \n     payloads: no scanned document images, no ID/passport content, no \n     password-vault data, no locked notes. Send only the minimal text the \n     user explicitly selects, and show a clear in-app notice.\n- Deterministic code does ALL calculations (debt math, forecasts, \n  statistics); the LLM only explains, drafts, parses, and summarizes.\n- Structured JSON outputs validated with Zod, timeouts, retries, prompt \n  versioning.\n- Prompt-injection defense: notes/documents are untrusted data and can \n  NEVER trigger actions without user confirmation.\n- Privacy: global \"disable AI\" toggle and per-module toggles, show what \n  is sent, never train on user data, log metadata (tokens, latency) not \n  content.\n- Deliver an \"Upgrade AI provider\" checklist: move to paid tier, set \n  free_tier=false, enable zero-retention terms, enable document AI, set \n  quotas per subscription plan, add budget alerts.\n- Semantic search/RAG: use Firestore vector search or on-device embeddings; \n  justify the choice and keep it scoped strictly per user.\n\n## Notifications\n- expo-notifications with LOCAL scheduled notifications (free, works \n  offline). FCM only for future remote push.\n\n## Documents and scanning\n- Camera document scanner with edge detection, crop, enhance, multi-page \n  to PDF; expo-image-manipulator, expo-document-picker, expo-image-picker, \n  expo-file-system. Prefer ON-DEVICE OCR (e.g., ML Kit).\n\n## Security on device\n- expo-local-authentication (biometric/PIN), expo-secure-store, optional \n  client-side encryption for sensitive files and notes.\n\n## Payments (future, inactive now)\n- Build an entitlement/plan abstraction and feature flags now. When \n  publishing, integrate RevenueCat (mobile) with Firebase entitlement \n  sync; web billing via Stripe only if needed. Do NOT integrate them yet.\n\n## Tooling\n- ESLint, Prettier, Husky, conventional commits, Jest, React Native \n  Testing Library, Maestro (E2E), Firebase Emulator Suite for local \n  testing and Security Rules unit tests, GitHub Actions CI, EAS Build/Update.\n\n# AUTH & ACCOUNTS\n- Google Sign-In only now. Use an AuthProvider abstraction with feature \n  flag \"auth.apple.enabled=false\" so Sign in with Apple can be added later \n  without refactoring. Document the exact steps (Apple Developer account, \n  Services ID, key, Firebase config) and note Apple requires it for \n  App Store apps offering third-party login.\n- Every user has a fully isolated account; all data syncs across devices.\n- Logout, session management, re-authentication for sensitive actions.\n- Account deletion that fully purges Firestore data and Storage files \n  (Cloud Function or Firebase extension), plus full data export \n  (JSON/ZIP). Required by stores and GDPR.\n\n# DATA MODEL & SYNC (Firestore)\n- All user data under users/{uid}/... subcollections (debts, payments, \n  reminders, tasks, notes, folders, documents, habits, transactions, \n  settings, aiUsage). No cross-user data access paths.\n- Design for Firestore\'s strengths: denormalize where it reduces reads, \n  maintain running totals (e.g., debt remaining balance, per-person \n  ledger totals) via transactions/batched writes, paginate everything, \n  and avoid unbounded listeners. Design indexes explicitly.\n- Document a conflict strategy per entity (updatedAt, version field, \n  soft deletes/tombstones, server timestamps), idempotent writes, and \n  how offline writes reconcile.\n- Provide a read/write cost estimate per typical user per day and \n  techniques to stay inside the free quota.\n- Optional end-to-end encryption mode for the Vault and secure notes \n  (user-held key, recovery key flow). Explain trade-offs, including that \n  AI cannot read E2E data unless the user opts in.\n\n# CORE FEATURES BY MODULE\nEach module has (A) standard features and (B) an embedded AI layer.\n\n## 1. Debts Manager\nA. Types: \"I owe\" / \"Owed to me\". Person (link to contacts), amount, \n   multi-currency (IQD, USD, EUR, custom) with optional exchange-rate \n   snapshot, start date, due date, optional interest, notes, attachments \n   (receipt/contract photo). Partial payments, full history, remaining \n   balance, statuses (active / partial / overdue / settled), installment \n   plans, per-person ledger, dashboard with totals per currency, charts.\nB. AI:\n   - Repayment prediction: probability a debt is repaid on time, computed \n     by deterministic statistics from that person\'s history; the LLM only \n     explains it. NEVER let the LLM do the math.\n   - Cash-flow forecast (\"you can afford to pay X by date Y\").\n   - Smart payoff plan: snowball vs. avalanche.\n   - Draft polite reminder messages to debtors (Arabic/Kurdish/English), \n     adjustable tone.\n   - Natural-language entry: \"Ahmed borrowed 200 dollars, due next month.\"\n   - Anomaly and risk alerts (e.g., growing overdue totals).\n\n## 2. Smart Reminders\nA. One-time, daily, weekly, monthly, yearly (birthdays), custom recurrence \n   (RRULE). Categories with distinct icons, colors, emoji, and Android \n   notification channels: Daily Routine, Birthday, Debt, Task, Medicine, \n   Bill, Document Expiry, Custom. Notification actions (Done, Snooze, \n   Open). Rich notifications (large icon/image attachments). Quiet hours. \n   Birthday import from contacts.\n   MANDATORY: a rolling-window scheduler that handles the iOS limit of 64 \n   pending local notifications, reschedules after reboot/app update/\n   timezone change, and is unit-tested. Explain Android/iOS/web \n   limitations honestly (e.g., per-notification custom icons are limited \n   on iOS; web scheduling is limited).\nB. AI:\n   - Natural-language scheduling (\"remind me every Sunday at 9am to call \n     mom\", \"birthday of Sara on 3 March\").\n   - Smart suggestions for optimal reminder times from completion behavior.\n   - Auto-categorization and icon selection.\n   - Detect schedule overload/conflicts and suggest rebalancing.\n\n## 3. To-Do & Planner\nA. Lists/projects, tasks, subtasks, priority, tags, due dates, recurrence, \n   drag-and-drop ordering, swipe actions, Today/Upcoming/Completed views, \n   unified calendar with reminders and debts, linked reminders.\nB. AI:\n   - Break a big goal into subtasks.\n   - Auto-prioritize and \"Plan my day/week\" from deadlines and effort.\n   - Parse tasks from free text or voice.\n   - Weekly review summary and productivity insights.\n\n## 4. Notes\nA. Rich-text/markdown notes, folders, tags, pinning, checklists, \n   attachments, color labels, search, per-note biometric lock, basic \n   version history, links to debts/tasks/documents.\nB. AI:\n   - Summarize, rewrite, translate, fix grammar, change tone.\n   - Extract action items and convert to tasks/reminders.\n   - Auto titles and tags.\n   - Ask questions across your notes (per-user semantic search).\n\n## 5. Document Vault\nA. Camera scanning (edge detection, perspective crop, enhance, multi-page \n   to PDF), upload from device, unlimited nested folders in each section, \n   rename/move/favorite/share, in-app preview, expiry dates with \n   reminders, per-folder biometric lock, compression, thumbnails, optional \n   client-side encryption, private storage only, short-lived download \n   URLs, never public.\nB. AI:\n   - OCR and searchable text (on-device preferred).\n   - Auto-classify (passport, ID, invoice, contract, receipt), suggest \n     folder + tags.\n   - Extract key fields (expiry date, amount, issuer) and offer to create \n     reminders or debt entries.\n   - Semantic search (\"find my car registration\").\n   Privacy: explicit per-feature consent, minimal data, and cloud document \n   AI DISABLED while the provider is on a free tier.\n\n## 6. Habits\nA. Daily/weekly habits, streaks, reminders, heatmaps, statistics, haptic \n   feedback on completion.\nB. AI: personalized suggestions, motivation/insights, analysis of why \n   streaks break, adaptive scheduling.\n\n## 7. Finance (Income/Expenses/Subscriptions)\nA. Transactions, categories, budgets, charts, recurring subscriptions with \n   renewal reminders, CSV import/export.\nB. AI: spending insights, budget recommendations, subscription waste \n   detection, receipt scanning to auto-create transactions, forecast.\n\n## 8. Global AI Assistant\n- Chat assistant accessible everywhere (command palette + floating button) \n  with tool calling across modules: \"What do I owe this month?\", \"Add a \n  task\", \"Show documents expiring soon\".\n- Any action that modifies data MUST show a confirmation preview first.\n- Daily briefing on the home screen (\"Today: 3 tasks, 1 birthday, \n  $200 due\").\n- Global search across all modules.\n\n# SECURITY & PRIVACY\n- Firestore and Storage Security Rules that enforce: users read/write \n  ONLY their own paths, field-level validation (types, sizes, required \n  fields), immutability of audit fields, and file type/size limits. \n  Write Security Rules unit tests using the Emulator Suite proving users \n  cannot access others\' data.\n- App Check enforced on Firestore, Storage, and AI Logic.\n- Biometric/PIN app lock, auto-lock timer, screenshot protection on \n  sensitive screens, secure clipboard handling, keys in Keychain/Keystore.\n- Zod validation on client AND in any Cloud Function, rate limiting and \n  abuse protection, CSP for web, dependency scanning, secrets management, \n  no secrets in the repo, separate Firebase projects for dev/staging/prod.\n- Compliance by design: GDPR/CCPA, privacy policy and ToS placeholders, \n  consent management (analytics, AI), data minimization, retention rules.\n- Provide a STRIDE-lite threat model and a security checklist.\n\n# MONETIZATION-READY DESIGN (inactive now)\n- Plans: Free, Pro, optional Family. Entitlements and limits (AI quota, \n  storage, document count, E2E vault, advanced analytics) driven by \n  Remote Config + a server-verified entitlements document; limits enforced \n  server-side (Rules/Functions), never only on the client.\n- Paywall screen, restore purchases, RevenueCat webhook -> Cloud Function \n  -> entitlements, all implemented as documented extension points only.\n- Provide a cost model: estimated cost per active user per month \n  (Firestore reads/writes, storage, AI) at 1k / 10k / 100k users, with \n  suggested pricing and the point where Firebase costs need optimization.\n\n# UI/UX STANDARDS\n- Premium, modern, minimal (Material You + iOS-level polish). Complete \n  design system: color tokens (light/dark/AMOLED), typography scale, \n  spacing, radius, elevation, iconography, motion guidelines.\n- Responsive: bottom tabs on mobile, sidebar + multi-pane on tablet/web.\n- Haptics on key interactions, skeleton loaders, optimistic updates, empty \n  states, onboarding, undo for destructive actions, pull-to-refresh, \n  quick-add FAB, home dashboard with AI daily briefing.\n- Accessibility (WCAG AA, screen readers, dynamic font sizes), full RTL, \n  localized dates/numbers/currencies.\n- Performance targets: cold start under 2s on mid-range Android, 60fps \n  lists, small bundle, image/PDF optimization.\n\n# ENGINEERING & SCALABILITY\n- Feature-based modular architecture (features/<module>/{ui,hooks,domain,\n  data}) with a shared repository layer; dependency boundaries enforced by \n  lint rules. Use a simple single repo (monorepo only if justified).\n- Environments: dev / staging / production Firebase projects, Remote \n  Config flags, OTA updates (EAS Update) with rollback.\n- Observability: Crashlytics, performance monitoring, structured logging, \n  budget alerts, opt-in analytics event plan.\n- Quality: strict TS, CI running lint/typecheck/tests/Rules tests, \n  coverage targets for core logic (debt math, recurrence engine, \n  notification scheduler, Security Rules).\n- Documentation: README, ADRs, data-model docs, runbooks (incident, \n  backup/restore). Enable scheduled Firestore exports/backups and test \n  the restore procedure.\n\n# DISTRIBUTION NOTES (explain honestly)\n- Android: APK via EAS Build, free for personal use.\n- iPhone: without a paid Apple Developer account, only short-lived dev \n  builds or the web app as a PWA; explain iOS PWA notification limits.\n- Web: Firebase Hosting free tier.\n\n# WORKING METHOD\n1. FIRST, do NOT write app code. Deliver: architecture overview, Mermaid \n   diagrams, Firestore data model with indexes, Security Rules design, \n   AI architecture, risk list (including Firebase lock-in, Firestore \n   cost traps, Storage/Blaze requirement, dev-build requirement), cost \n   estimate, and a phased roadmap. Ask up to 5 critical clarifying \n   questions and wait for my answers.\n2. Then work in small phases, each ending with working, tested, runnable \n   code and exact commands:\n   - Phase 1: Project setup, EAS dev build, Firebase projects, design \n     system, navigation, Google login (Apple behind a flag), Firestore + \n     offline persistence, repository layer, Security Rules + tests, App \n     Check, AI Logic skeleton (provider abstraction, quotas, privacy \n     gate), CI.\n   - Phase 2: Debts module (+ AI).\n   - Phase 3: Reminders + notification engine (+ AI).\n   - Phase 4: To-do/Planner and Notes (+ AI).\n   - Phase 5: Document Vault + scanning (+ AI).\n   - Phase 6: Habits, Finance, Global Assistant.\n   - Phase 7: Entitlements/paywall scaffolding, hardening, performance, \n     release prep.\n3. After each phase give: what was built, how to test it, known \n   limitations, security considerations, and the next step. Wait for my \n   approval before continuing.\n4. Write COMPLETE, working code (no pseudocode, no \"etc.\"). Explain any \n   platform limitation honestly.\n5. If any requirement conflicts with cost, security, or platform rules, \n   say so and propose the best alternative.\n\nBegin with Step 1 (architecture and planning deliverables)."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "<clarifying-answers>\nQ: This workspace runs a live Vite web preview rather than native Expo/iOS/Android build tools. How should we build Phase 1?\nA: Live React Web/PWA here with Expo-portable core\nQ: Cloud Storage requires Firebase Blaze (pay-as-you-go with free allowances). Which Stage 0 storage posture do you prefer?\nA: Spark free tier with Local-Only Vault first\nQ: Which primary language and default currency should Stage 0 optimize first while keeping EN, AR, and Kurdish Sorani RTL ready?\nA: English UI with IQD and USD dual currency\nQ: How should client-side E2E encryption work for your sensitive Vault documents and locked notes?\nA: Opt-in per folder/note with recovery key\nQ: What default visual direction should LifeOS use across its light, dark, and AMOLED themes?\nA: Calm slate and emerald Material You + iOS polish\n</clarifying-answers>"
