  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Observing Network Configuration**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the network configuration aspects. The key is in understanding how the WebSocket connection is set up, given that `socketProtocol` seems to be conditionally determined based on the importMetaUrl.protocol. I\'m noting the significance of the `hmrPort` and the structure of the `socketHost` string template.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Root Cause**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the core issue. The problem stems from the hardcoded `hmrPort` (24678) used for the WebSocket connection. The browser on Cloud Run attempts to connect to a port that\'s never open (wss://[domain]:24678). Even with WebSocket support on port 443, that port 24678 is always closed. The key lies in correctly configuring the WebSocket server with the appropriate HTTP server instance.\n\n\n**Verifying Configurations & Logic**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got a handle on the conditional logic. If `env.DISABLE_HMR` is not \'true\', things should work flawlessly. Importantly, with `isWsServerSpecified` being true, setting `hmrPort` to `null` is the key fix that ensures the connection uses the same port as the HTTP server. Even more importantly, If `serverConfig.hmr` is `false`, and `serverConfig.ws` is `{ server: httpServer }`, the WebSocket server is correctly attached. This means WebSocket connections work *perfectly* when editing files! And, with `DISABLE_HMR` not \'true\', then WebSockets connect, AND HMR works cleanly without the port 24678 issue.\n\n\n**Reviewing Edge Case Scenarios**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a critical edge case. Even with the recent fixes, I\'m considering what could go wrong if the reverse proxy on Cloud Run blocks WebSocket upgrades on the root path `/` despite `DISABLE_HMR` being true. I\'m trying to recall the root cause of \"error 0\" on the Cloud Run URL and to review the error stack trace again. The goal is to ensure a fully robust solution.\n\n\n**Analyzing Error Conditions**\n\nI\'m now digging into specific error scenarios to ensure full robustness. I\'ve re-examined the root cause of \"error 0\", which stems from the hardcoded `hmrPort`. The `client.mjs` code rethrows the websocket error directly as an unhandled rejection when the port is incorrect. However, I\'ve just realized that if `DISABLE_HMR` is true and Cloud Run correctly proxies WebSockets, the connection should work flawlessly because the `hmrPort` will correctly be null.\n\n\n"
