  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing Phase 3**\n\nI\'m deep in designing Phase 3, giving it the architectural rigor it deserves. Mapping out every requirement from the product vision and specs is my current focus. I\'m aiming for honesty and visual polish in every aspect of the smart reminders and notification scheduler design.\n\n\n**Defining Reminder Schema**\n\nI\'ve been working on the `ReminderItem` schema. It\'s now synchronized across multiple files: the Firebase blueprint, Firestore rules, and the Expo-portable reminder schemas. The schema includes owner UID, title, notes, category, priority (with quiet hours override), due date as a strict ISO-8601 timestamp, and an optional RFC-5545 recurrence rule. I\'m focusing on rigorous data validation for each field.\n\n\n**Refining Reminder State**\n\nI\'m now refining the `ReminderItem` schema with additional fields. I\'ve added `snoozedUntilIso`, `respectQuietHours`, `status`, `completedCount`, `lastCompletedAtIso`, `linkedDebtId`, `version`, `createdAt`, and `updatedAt`. I\'m ensuring data validation across the schema, and maintaining synchronization with the existing blueprint where necessary, for seamless compatibility.\n\n\n**Implementing Recurrence Rules**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in implementing RFC-5545 recurrence rules. This includes the following: parsing, human-readable summaries, and deterministic occurrence expansion. The core focus is handling month-end clamping and sorting weekly occurrences. I am working on the logic for computing the next occurrence after a reminder is completed.\n\n\n\n**Scheduling Notifications**\n\nI\'m deep in the specifics of the notification scheduler. I\'ve designed `shiftPastQuietHours`, which handles the logic for postponing reminders that occur during quiet hours. I\'m also now implementing the `buildRollingNotificationQueue`, explicitly accounting for iOS\'s 64-notification limit. This queue takes active reminders, quiet hours, debt alerts, and the current time, expanding occurrences, applying overrides, and sorting them. The goal is to deterministically slice the results to 64 slots, returning important metrics.\n\n\n**Analyzing Workload Overload**\n\nI\'m now implementing the workload analyzer. It\'s designed to detect days with heavy reminder loads or clustered events, suggesting a rebalance. I\'m focusing on on-device processing and deterministic calculations for efficiency. The goal is simple: no AI is needed for overload analysis. I\'m also ensuring honest notification delivery across web and native platforms. On web, I\'m integrating with the browser\'s Notification API and explaining the limitations of background wake-ups.\n\n\n**Implementing Local Offline Storage**\n\nI\'m now implementing `LocalOfflineRemindersRepository`, a localStorage-backed store for offline use. This allows immediate Stage 0 functionality, even without authentication or mocked data. I\'m focusing on strict Zod validation, the `handleFirestoreError` function, and using `serverTimestamp()` for updates. I\'m also ensuring the implementation is action-split compliant.\n\nI\'m developing a React hook, `useReminders.ts`, which provides real-time reminders and computations for a 64-slot rolling queue synchronized with the user\'s quiet hours, alongside CRUD actions, and browser API permission checks.\n\n\n**Enforcing Privacy & Quotas**\n\nI\'m working on a client wrapper within `src/core/ai/remindersAiClient.ts` to enforce a strict Privacy Gate and track quotas for AI interactions. This ensures responsible and controlled use of the AI features. I\'m focusing on robust measures to protect user data and manage resource consumption.\n\n**Streamlining Firestore Rules**\n\nI am now updating the `ReminderItem` in `/firebase-blueprint.json` and drafting `/DRAFT_firestore.rules`. My current focus is implementing enriched validation rules (`isValidReminderItem`, action-split `allow update`), ensuring strictness with `hasAll`/`hasOnly`, validating `isValidIsoTimestamp`, enforcing `version` control, and maintaining immutability for `uid` and `createdAt`. I\'m rigorously running ESLint to eliminate errors.\n\n\n**Designing UI Layout**\n\nI\'m now designing the user interface layout. I\'m focusing on a clean segmented tab structure for the core views, including an **Agenda** with filters, an **8 Channels** directory, the **64-Slot Scheduler** inspector, and a **Workload Balance** view. Each reminder row needs fast actions for completion, snoozing, editing, and deletion with undo. I am focusing on the \"Due Today,\" \"Overdue,\" \"Recurring Active,\" and \"Rolling 64-Slot Queue\" KPI strip for at-a-glance information, and an embedded AI Quick Schedule box on the `ReminderFormModal.tsx`.\n\n\n"
    message_id: "Uq4Ea4aQIICs_PUP3IS_sQU"
    tool_calls {
